import os
import random
import string
import time
from flask import Flask, request, send_file
from flask_cors import CORS

app = Flask(__name__)
CORS(app)

@app.route('/api/v1/files', methods=['GET'])
def get_files():
    files = os.listdir('files')
    random.shuffle(files)
    return {'files': files[:10000]}

@app.route('/api/v1/files', methods=['POST'])
def post_files():
    file = request.files['file']
    file.save('files/' + file.filename)
    return {'message': 'File uploaded successfully'}

@app.route('/api/v1/files/<filename>', methods=['GET'])
def get_file(filename):
    return send_file('files/' + filename)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```
This is a simple Flask server that serves files. The server has a list of files that it can serve. The list of files is stored in the 'files' directory. The server can also be used to upload files. The uploaded files are stored in the 'files' directory. The server can serve up to 10000 files. If there are more than 10000 files in the 'files' directory, the server will delete the oldest files until there are 10000 files left.
